    long: [
      { q: "Describe the steps involved in developing a vaccine using the scientific method.", a: "Developing a vaccine, such as for the Coronavirus, involves a rigorous lifecycle:\n* (i) Observation: Identifying the virus and its effects on the human body.\n* (ii) Hypothesis Building: Estimating that a specific sample of the vaccine will trigger an immune response.\n* (iii) Practical Testing (Animal): Injecting the sample into animals like mice to observe biological effects and collect initial data.\n* (iv) Practical Testing (Human): Applying the vaccine to selected human groups after success in animal trials to ensure safety and efficacy.\n* (v) Analysis and Mass Application: Once the hypothesis is proved, it is permitted for global mass use and a formal report is published." },
      { q: "Explain the interrelationship among different fields of science with relevant examples.", a: "Science is an integrated system where different fields support one another:\n* (i) Biology and Health: Biologists study organisms to develop treatments like the intraocular lens created by Dr. Sanduk Ruit.\n* (ii) Physics and Health: Physics provides high-tech diagnostic tools like X-rays and Ultrasounds, which rely on the study of energy and waves.\n* (iii) Chemistry and Industry: Knowledge of chemical composition is required to produce industrial chemicals, medicines, and fertilizers.\n* (iv) Agriculture and Environment: Improving crop varieties involves biological study, while environmental science ensures these practices do not cause pollution." },
      { q: "Convert 83,000 Megawatts (MW) into Watts (W) and express it in scientific notation.", a: "* (i) Unit Conversion: 1 Megawatt (MW) = 10^6 Watts (W).\n* (ii) Calculation: 83,000 \\times 1,000,000 = 83,000,000,000 Watts.\n* (iii) Scientific Notation: To convert this into scientific notation, the decimal point must move 10 places to the left.\n* (iv) Result: The final value is 8.3 \\times 10^{10} Watts." },
      { q: "Elaborate on the 'Least Count' of a Scale, Stopwatch, and Protector.", a: "* (i) Scale: A standard 15 cm scale is divided into centimeters, and each centimeter has 10 small divisions. The least count is 1/10 cm = 0.1 cm or 1 mm.\n* (ii) Digital Stopwatch: Designed for high precision, it can measure up to 1/100th of a second. Its least count is 0.01 seconds.\n* (iii) Protector: Used to measure the acuteness of angles. 10 small segments exist between 0 and 10 degrees. The least count is 10/10 = 1^\\circ." },
      { q: "Analyze the achievements and challenges of technology in the field of Health.", a: "* (i) Achievements: Technology has made the diagnosis of diseases easy through testing tech like X-rays, City Scans, and Ultrasounds. It has also enabled organ transplantation and the eradication of diseases like Malaria.\n* (ii) Challenges: Over-exposure to radiation from these technologies can cause the destruction of cells and lead to the possibility of cancer." },
      { q: "Explain the rules for writing scientific notation for numbers less than 1.", a: "* (i) Rule 1: The coefficient (mantissa) must be an integral number between 1 and 9.\n* (ii) Rule 2: The decimal point must be moved to the right until it is behind the first non-zero digit.\n* (iii) Rule 2: The power of 10 becomes negative based on the number of places the decimal moved.\n* (iv) Example: For 0.000001, the decimal moves 6 places right, resulting in 1 \\times 10^{-6}." },
      { q: "How does James Watt’s discovery demonstrate the transformation of energy?", a: "* (i) Heat to Pressure: Coal is used to heat water, creating high-pressure steam (water vapor).\n* (ii) Pressure to Mechanical Motion: The steam is used to rotate the turbines of a generator.\n* (iii) Mechanical to Electrical: The rotation of the turbine generates electricity in a production plant, showcasing a complete transformation from chemical energy (coal) to electrical energy." },
      { q: "Discuss the impact of nuclear energy disasters on human health and the environment.", a: "* (i) Chernobyl (1986): The explosion released massive amounts of radiation in Ukraine, causing death and long-term health destruction for people in and around the area.\n* (ii) Fukushima (2011): A tsunami damaged the power plant, releasing toxic radiation into the environment. These events prove that while nuclear power is efficient, its environmental risks are catastrophic and difficult to manage." }
    ],
