    vshort: [
      { q: "What is Taxonomy?", a: "The scientific study of identification, nomenclature, and classification of living organisms is called Taxonomy." },
      { q: "Define Species.", a: "A group of living organisms with similar characteristics that can interbreed among themselves to produce fertile offspring is called Species." },
      { q: "What is meant by Genus?", a: "A group of different species that are closely related and share many common characteristics is called Genus." },
      { q: "By what title is Carolus Linnaeus known?", a: "He is known as the 'Father of Modern Taxonomy'." },
      { q: "Who is the founder of the Five Kingdom classification system?", a: "American taxonomist Robert Harding Whittaker." },
      { q: "Write the scientific name of paddy.", a: "The scientific name of paddy is Oryza sativa." },
      { q: "What are Prokaryotes?", a: "Simple organisms that do not have a well-defined nucleus and membrane-bound organelles in their cells are called Prokaryotes." },
      { q: "What is Lichen?", a: "Lichen is the mutual symbiotic relationship between algae (Algae) and fungi (Fungi)." },
      { q: "Where is Mycelium found?", a: "Mycelium is found in the body of fungi (Fungi)." },
      { q: "In what form do fungi store food?", a: "Fungi store food in the form of Glycogen." },
      { q: "Give any two examples of Kingdom Monera.", a: "Bacteria and Blue-green algae (Cyanobacteria)." },
      { q: "What type of cells are organisms of Kingdom Protista made of?", a: "They are made of eukaryotic (Eukaryotic) cells." },
      { q: "To which kingdom does Mucor belong?", a: "Mucor belongs to kingdom 'Fungi'." },
      { q: "What does the first word of the Binomial system represent?", a: "The first word represents Genus." },
      { q: "Write the scientific name of cow.", a: "The scientific name of cow is Bos taurus." },
      { q: "Slime mold is an organism of which kingdom?", a: "It is an organism of kingdom Protista." },
      { q: "In whose cell wall is Peptidoglycan found?", a: "It is found in the cell wall of bacteria (Monera)." },
      { q: "How does Euglena make food?", a: "Euglena makes food by performing photosynthesis with the help of chloroplast." },
      { q: "Write the scientific name of cat.", a: "The scientific name of cat is Felis catus." },
      { q: "Which is the largest level of modern classification?", a: "The largest level of modern classification is Kingdom." }
    ],
    short: [
      { q: "Why is the binomial system of nomenclature necessary?", a: "Across the world, the same organism is known by different names according to local languages, which creates confusion in scientific study. The binomial nomenclature system provides a single scientific name to an organism globally, making the study simple and systematic." },
      { q: "Write any four importances of classifying living organisms.", a: "The four importances of classification are as follows:\n(i) It helps in understanding the origin and evolution of living organisms.\n(ii) It makes it easy to obtain a lot of information about millions of organisms in a short time.\n(iii) It provides a basis for the scientific naming of organisms.\n(iv) It clarifies the mutual relationship and diversity among different organisms." },
      { q: "What are the main bases of the Five Kingdom classification?", a: "R.H. Whittaker used the following bases:\n(i) Cell structure (Prokaryotic or Eukaryotic)\n(ii) Body organization or complexity (Unicellular or Multicellular)\n(iii) Presence and structure of cell wall\n(iv) Mode of nutrition (Autotrophic, Heterotrophic, or Decomposer)" },
      { q: "Mention the main characteristics of Kingdom Monera.", a: "The characteristics of Kingdom Monera are as follows:\n(i) These are microscopic and prokaryotic organisms.\n(ii) They do not have a well-defined nucleus and developed organelles in their cells.\n(iii) Genetic material in the form of DNA is scattered in the cytoplasm of the cell.\n(iv) They can be autotrophic, parasitic, or symbiotic." },
      { q: "Why is Kingdom Protista called a 'diverse' kingdom?", a: "Kingdom Protista is considered diverse because it contains unicellular eukaryotic organisms that share characteristics with all three groups: animal-like (Protozoa), plant-like (algae/Euglena), and fungi-like (slime molds)." },
      { q: "Why are fungi (Fungi) called decomposers (Decomposers)?", a: "Fungi secrete enzymes to chemically decompose dead and decaying organic matter into simpler elements and mix them into the soil. Since they obtain their nutrition through this process, they are called decomposers." },
      { q: "How is Euglena different from other animals?", a: "Although Euglena has a flagellum for locomotion like animals, it contains chloroplasts like plants, which allows it to perform photosynthesis and make its own food. This double characteristic is not present in other animals." },
      { q: "Write the hierarchical levels of taxonomy in order.", a: "The taxonomic levels from top to bottom are as follows:\n1. Kingdom\n2. Phylum/Division\n3. Class\n4. Order\n5. Family\n6. Genus\n7. Species" },
      { q: "Explain Symbiotic Fungi with an example.", a: "Symbiosis is the process in which two organisms of different species live together by depending on each other so that both benefit. An example is 'Lichen', where fungi provide shelter and moisture to algae, and algae perform photosynthesis to make food for fungi." },
      { q: "What is the difference between Saprotrophic and Parasitic Fungi?", a: "Saprotrophic Fungi obtain nutrition from dead and decaying matter (e.g., Mushroom), while Parasitic Fungi live on or inside the body of another living organism to obtain nutrition and usually cause diseases (e.g., Puccinia)." },
      { q: "Why is Species considered an important unit in modern classification?", a: "Because Species is the smallest and most specific level of classification. Organisms in it share the maximum number of characteristics and can naturally interbreed to produce fertile offspring." },
      { q: "What is the difference between Archaebacteria and Eubacteria?", a: "Archaebacteria are ancient bacteria that can survive in extreme environments like high temperatures or salinity. Eubacteria are known as 'true bacteria' and have cell walls made of peptidoglycan." },
      { q: "Why are scientific names italicized or underlined?", a: "Scientific names are mostly derived from the Latin language. To distinguish these names from ordinary words and to follow international rules, they are italicized when typed and underlined when written by hand." },
      { q: "How is 'ecological role' addressed in the Five Kingdom system?", a: "In this system, based on the mode of nutrition, organisms are clearly divided into producers (Plante), consumers (Animelia), and decomposers (Fungi), which helps in understanding the balance of the ecosystem." },
      { q: "How are unicellular organisms organized in Whittaker's classification?", a: "Whittaker organized them by placing prokaryotic unicellular organisms in Kingdom 'Monera' and eukaryotic unicellular organisms in Kingdom 'Protista'." }
    ],
