    reasons: [
      { q: "Carolus Linnaeus is called the 'Father of Modern Taxonomy'.", a: "Reason: He was the first to prepare a scientific and systematic framework for the classification of living organisms and the Binomial Nomenclature system." },
      { q: "Fungi are called decomposers.", a: "Reason: They decompose dead and decaying organic matter into simpler forms through chemical processes, mixing them with the soil and obtaining their nutrition." },
      { q: "The Five Kingdom classification system is considered more scientific than the Two Kingdom system.", a: "Reason: It clearly separates prokaryotic, eukaryotic, unicellular, and multicellular organisms based on cell structure, body organization, and mode of nutrition." },
      { q: "Amoeba and bacteria are both unicellular but are kept in different kingdoms.", a: "Reason: Bacteria is a prokaryotic (without definite nucleus) organism kept in Monera, whereas Amoeba is a eukaryotic (with definite nucleus) organism kept in Protista." },
      { q: "Euglena is called a plant-like protist.", a: "Reason: Its cell contains chloroplasts, which allow it to make its own food through photosynthesis." },
      { q: "Species is called the basic unit of classification.", a: "Reason: It is the smallest group of classification whose members can breed among themselves to produce fertile offspring." },
      { q: "Mushrooms do not contain chloroplasts.", a: "Reason: Mushroom is a decomposer (Fungi) and obtains prepared food by absorbing it from decaying organic matter, so it does not need chloroplasts to perform photosynthesis." },
      { q: "Both fungi and algae benefit in Lichen.", a: "Reason: Fungi provide protection and moisture to algae, while algae perform photosynthesis and provide food to fungi, making their coexistence possible." },
      { q: "Bacteria can survive in extreme conditions.", a: "Reason: Especially Archaebacteria have a unique cell wall and protein structure that allows them to survive in extreme environments like high temperatures or high salinity." },
      { q: "In scientific names, the first letter of Genus is written in Capital.", a: "Reason: It is a rule of the internationally accepted Binomial Nomenclature system, which helps to clearly distinguish the generic name from the specific name." }
    ],
    long: [
      { q: "What is classification of living beings? Explain its importance in five points.", a: "The scientific process of dividing living beings into different groups based on similarities and dissimilarities in their physical structure, habitat, and biological processes is called classification.\nIts importance is as follows:\n(i) It makes the study of millions of organisms systematic and possible in a short time.\n(ii) It clarifies the evolutionary relationships and history among organisms.\n(iii) It helps in identifying any newly discovered organism and placing it in a specific group.\n(iv) It helps in naming organisms scientifically so they are recognized by the same name worldwide.\n(v) It provides a scientific basis and structural framework for the study of biology." },
      { q: "Mention the bases of the Five Kingdom classification system and write the names of all five kingdoms.", a: "Robert Whittaker proposed the Five Kingdom classification system in 1969. The main bases of this system are as follows:\n(i) Cell type (Prokaryotic or Eukaryotic)\n(ii) Body organization or complexity (Unicellular or Multicellular)\n(iii) Mode of nutrition (Autotrophic, Decomposer, or Consumer)\n(iv) Presence and structure of cell wall\nThe five kingdoms are:\n1. Kingdom Monera\n2. Kingdom Protista\n3. Kingdom Fungi\n4. Kingdom Plante\n5. Kingdom Animelia" },
      { q: "Write the scientific classification of Human in all seven levels in order.", a: "The scientific classification of human is as follows:\nKingdom: Animelia\nPhylum: Chordata\nClass: Mammalia\nOrder: Primates\nFamily: Hominidae\nGenus: Homo\nSpecies: sapiens\nScientific Name: <i>Homo sapiens</i>" },
      { q: "Describe the main characteristics of Kingdom Monera and its types with examples.", a: "The main characteristics of Kingdom Monera are:\n(i) Microscopic organisms made of prokaryotic cells.\n(ii) Lack a definite nucleus and developed cell organelles.\n(iii) DNA is present as genetic material.\n(iv) Can survive in harsh environments.\nIts three types are:\nArchaebacteria: Ancient bacteria (e.g., Pyrolobus)\nEubacteria: True bacteria (e.g., Azotobacter)\nCyanobacteria: Blue-green algae (e.g., Nostoc)" },
      { q: "Explain the three types of Kingdom Protista with examples.", a: "Kingdom Protista contains eukaryotic unicellular organisms. Its three types are:\n(i) Animal-like Protists: They lack cell walls and are heterotrophic. Examples: Amoeba, Paramecium.\n(ii) Plant-like Protists: They contain chloroplasts and make their own food. Examples: Euglena, Diatoms.\n(iii) Fungi-like Protists: They have characteristics similar to fungi but are unicellular. Example: Slime mold." },
      { q: "Classify fungi (Fungi) on the basis of nutrition with examples.", a: "On the basis of nutrition, fungi are classified into three types:\n(i) Saprotrophic Fungi: They obtain nutrition from dead and decaying matter. Examples: Mushroom, Mucor.\n(ii) Parasitic Fungi: They obtain nutrition from living plants or animals and cause diseases. Example: Puccinia.\n(iii) Symbiotic Fungi: They live in association with other organisms, benefiting both. Example: Lichen." },
      { q: "Write the rules of the Binomial Nomenclature system in detail.", a: "The rules of Binomial Nomenclature developed by Carolus Linnaeus are as follows:\n(i) Each organism must have two words in its scientific name: the first is Genus and the second is Species.\n(ii) The first letter of the Genus name must be Capital, and the first letter of the Species name must be Small.\n(iii) These names should generally be in the Latin language.\n(iv) Both names must be underlined separately when handwritten, or written in Italics when typed." },
      { q: "Mention all the levels of classification of Mustard.", a: "The classification of mustard (Brassica campestris) is as follows:\nKingdom: Plante\nDivision: Tracheophytes\nClass: Angiosperm\nOrder: Brassicales\nFamily: Brassicaceae\nGenus: Brassica\nSpecies: campestris" }
    ],
