    reasons: [
      { q: "Mushroom is called a saprophyte.", a: "Reason: Since it lacks chlorophyll, it cannot perform photosynthesis and depends on decaying matter for food." },
      { q: "Wild mushrooms should not be eaten.", a: "Reason: Most wild mushrooms can be poisonous, and their identification is not possible without laboratory testing." },
      { q: "Mushroom is a good food for heart patients.", a: "Reason: The cholesterol content in mushrooms is zero, which prevents blockage in blood vessels and keeps the heart healthy." },
      { q: "Straw is boiled for 15-20 minutes in mushroom cultivation.", a: "Reason: It is done to destroy harmful microbes present in the straw and to make it soft for mushroom growth." },
      { q: "The bag of oyster mushroom is kept in a dark room for the first 7 days.", a: "Reason: A dark and humid environment is required for the development of mushroom mycelium (spawn running)." },
      { q: "Mushroom is called the 'scavenger' of the environment.", a: "Reason: It decomposes dead and decaying matter and mixes it with the soil, helping to clean the environment." },
      { q: "The protein content in mushrooms is considered high.", a: "Reason: On a dry weight basis, it contains 19 to 35 percent protein, which is much higher compared to rice and wheat." },
      { q: "Morel mushrooms are very expensive.", a: "Reason: They are rare and rich in medicinal properties, with high demand and low supply in the international market." },
      { q: "It is necessary to make holes in the mushroom bag.", a: "Reason: Holes are made in the plastic bag to provide oxygen for the respiration of mushroom seeds (spawn)." },
      { q: "Red mushroom is considered a medicinal mushroom.", a: "Reason: Special substances found in it help in manufacturing medicines used for treating serious diseases like cancer and asthma." }
    ],
    long: [
      { q: "Draw a labeled diagram of the structure of a mushroom and describe its main parts.", a: "The body of a mushroom is divided into two parts:\n(1) Mycelium: This is the thread-like part under the soil that absorbs nutrition.\n(2) Fruiting Body: This is the part above the ground that consists of stipe (stalk), pileus (umbrella-like part), and gills (where spores are formed). The pileus protects the gills, and the gills produce spores." },
      { q: "Explain the life cycle of a mushroom in detail.", a: "The life cycle of a mushroom begins with basidiospores. Under favorable conditions, spores germinate to form primary mycelium. The fusion of two primary mycelia of different strains forms secondary (dikaryotic) mycelium. Later, the fruiting body of the mushroom develops from this. In the gills of the fruiting body, meiosis division occurs to produce basidiospores again, and this cycle continues." },
      { q: "Describe in detail the technology of cultivating oyster mushrooms.", a: "For oyster mushroom cultivation, good straw is first selected. The straw is cut into small pieces, boiled, and dried. Then, straw and mushroom seeds (spawn) are placed in layers inside a plastic bag. Holes are made in the bag, and it is kept in a dark room. Once the mycelium spreads, the plastic is removed and water is sprayed regularly. The mushroom is ready for harvesting in about 25 days." },
      { q: "Discuss the nutritional and medicinal value of mushrooms.", a: "Mushrooms contain high-quality protein, vitamin B_2, B_{12}, and minerals. They boost immunity. Medicinally, they help in controlling diabetes, high blood pressure, and cancer. Important medicines are manufactured from species like red mushrooms." },
      { q: "Compare the modern and traditional methods of storing mushrooms.", a: "Traditionally, mushrooms are dried under the sun to make dry slices (sukuti). In the modern method, a solution of potassium bisulphide (1\\%) and citric acid (0.2\\%) is used. In addition, mushrooms are dried in a modern oven at 40-45^\\circ\\text{C} to maintain their quality." },
      { q: "Write the functional role of the layers in the gills of a mushroom.", a: "The gills consist of trama, sub-hymenium, and hymenium layers. Trama provides the structural framework to the gills. Sub-hymenium provides nutrition to the cells of the hymenium. The basidium is present in the hymenium, where reproduction takes place and basidiospores are formed." },
      { q: "What precautions should be taken while cultivating mushrooms?", a: "Special attention should be paid to cleanliness during cultivation. The straw must be properly sterilized. The room temperature and moisture must be balanced. Hands must be clean when sowing seeds and harvesting mushrooms to prevent the spread of other harmful fungi." },
      { q: "How can mushroom cultivation help in poverty alleviation in Nepal?", a: "Mushroom cultivation can be done with low investment in a small space. It provides self-employment to women and unemployed youth. Selling excess mushrooms in the market as dried slices or pickles provides good income, which strengthens the rural economy." }
    ],
